  "disclosures": [
    "the output is a slice of the record the table selected; the runner records the span and re-checks it byte by byte, so L1/L2 measure the slice, not the intent.",
    "abstention is a reading: no_anchor, ambiguous_candidates and coverage never gate the output and never enter the verdict.",
    "the four synonym rewrites change bytes in the middle of the record, which breaks contiguous containment by definition; they remain in the denominator.",
    "chunks shorter than 16 bytes are dropped after the cut set is fixed, so their bytes are invisible to the address; the rule never moves a cut point.",
    "the band is six bytes, the predicate's own dependency width, and only the final chunk is dropped; both are the smallest correct choice, not tuned values.",
    "a one-block identity certifies at least 16 verbatim interior bytes, not the whole record: that is the specificity a short record can offer, and it is reported, not hidden. An empty identity would anchor every query and is rejected.",
    "cut points are byte offsets; a fallback slice can therefore start inside a multi-byte UTF-8 character. Such a slice is still a legal, byte-faithful slice under the frozen definition (span inside the record, length matching), but it is not decodable text. The count is reported (layers.utf8_slices); the three synonym rewrites that break containment fall back to a partial span and are the scored rows concerned. Character-aligned slicing is a representation-layer decision, not part of this gate.",
    "the fallback label (fallback / fallback_reason / overlap_ratio) is a reading; it never decides whether an output is produced, and it adds no threshold.",
    "the legacy column is a frozen copy of the old stride-2 / DF=1 / DF=0-guard read rule and never enters the verdict.",
    "the corpus snapshot is what the run reads, so later plan edits cannot move the acceptance set."
  ]
